[%think 3805] [%out 4121] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 2393] The
position already favors Black. Black's bishop and four pawns face my knight and
two pawns. Black's a2 pawn is stopped only because my king holds a1. -
**Nxd5:** after Kxd5 it becomes a king-and-pawn ending. My king would take a2,
but Black's king goes to f3 and wins the f2 pawn. Black then has three pawns
against my one, which is lost for me. - **Taking a2 or other king moves:** Kxa2
is illegal because the bishop on d5 guards a2. Most other king moves let the
pawn promote on a1. - **Nd7:** this position has now occurred twice. If Black
answers Ke4 again, Nf6+ Kd4 repeats it a third time and the game is drawn. If
Black plays Kd3 instead, Ne5+ checks the king and also attacks f7 and g6, so I
still have counterplay. Going for the repetition draw is the practical choice.
